<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory of Tours</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 538–594</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> was a Gallo-Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Tours from 573 and the author of the Histories, often called the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. His work is the main source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> and the Merovingian kings, and for many small kingdoms and rulers of 5th- and 6th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> that are otherwise almost unknown. He also records events such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hygelac">Hygelac</a>'s raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours.jpg" alt="Gregory of Tours" width="320" height="711" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_of_Tours_cour_Napoleon_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gregory was born Georgius Florentius at Clermont in the Auvergne, into the upper stratum of Gallo-Roman society. His father Florentius was a senator of Clermont, and relatives of his held the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> at the time of his birth. After his father's death he was raised by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Gallus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Clermont, and by Gallus's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, who ordained him deacon. A serious illness led him to visit the tomb of Saint Martin at Tours, and he recovered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> fell vacant, the clergy and people chose Gregory as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Deputies overtook him at the court of King Sigebert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, and he was consecrated on 22 August 573, at the age of 34, though he accepted much against his will. He spent most of his career at Tours, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, where Frankish influence from the north met Gallo-Roman influence from the south. As the centre of the cult of Saint Martin it was a pilgrimage site and a political sanctuary. He dealt with four Frankish kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilperic-i">Chilperic I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> and Childebert II. After rumours that he had slandered Chilperic's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a>, Chilperic had him arrested and tried for treason.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His main work, the Decem Libri Historiarum or Ten Books of Histories, known as the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, begins with the Creation and moves quickly to the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> and the history of the Frankish kings. Books V to X, covering the years from 575 to 591, rest largely on his own knowledge. He added an epilogue in 594. He wrote in Late Latin and apologised for his rough style. He opens with a statement of his Catholic orthodoxy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>. Recent scholarship holds that his underlying purpose was to set the vanity of secular life against the miracles of the saints.</p><p>He also wrote the Life of the Fathers, twenty lives of holy men, and the Book of the Glories of the Martyrs, begun in 587, which tie saints and relics to local shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Historians value the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> as the central source for the Franks' rise, and it is often compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gregory of Tours” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gregory of Tours</b> speaks of a single Alemannic king killed in battle with Clovis, which may indicate greater unity by the late 5th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gregory of Tours</b> wrote his Histories, the main narrative of the early Merovingian kingdom, in the late sixth century, and Einhard’s Life of Charlemagne became a model for royal biography.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The raid of King Hygelac into Frisia is mentioned by <b>Gregory of Tours</b> and can be dated to around 521.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gregory of Tours</b> records the counts Macliau and his son Waroch fighting for control of Vannes.</blockquote><a href="/state/broerec">Broërec</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 464 his son Syagrius held the region from Soissons; <b>Gregory of Tours</b> calls him king of the Romans, though he probably never used the title.</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-soissons">Domain of Soissons</a></li><li><blockquote>Known almost only from <b>Gregory of Tours</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cambrai">Kingdom of Cambrai</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont-Ferrand</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.78,3.09&amp;year=574" aria-label="Clermont-Ferrand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gregory of Tours"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5184 6199h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5184 6199h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.78,3.09&amp;year=574">What was here in 574 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Woe to our day, since the pursuit of letters has perished from among us and no one can be found among the people who can set forth the deeds of the present on the written page.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Franks, first preface &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1916 &#183; the lament he heard around him, which he gives as the reason for writing &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74955/pg74955-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…although my speech is rude, I have been unable to be silent as to the struggles between the wicked and the upright…&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Franks, first preface &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1916 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74955/pg74955-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it has often been said by men of our day, that few understand the learned words of the rhetorician but many the rude language of the common people.&#8221;</p><footer>History of the Franks, first preface &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1916 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74955/pg74955-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
